									<div class="blog-content">
										<h2 class="blog-title">
											<a href="/blog/5-tips-to-market-your-business-on-youtube-to-grow-followers-and-subscriptions">5 Tips to Market Your Business on YouTube to Grow Followers & Subscriptions</a>
										</h2>
                                        <div class="blog-alt-text">
                                          	<p>If you're an entrepreneur with a business to promote, it's only natural that you'd turn to YouTube. Not only does it have the most views of any social media network (1 billion hours watched per day), but it also has a huge audience of active users who are hungry for business-related content.</p>
<p>But if you want your videos to stand out on a platform with so many competitors and viewers, then you need to do more than just upload them: You need to make sure they're being discovered by your target audience. You would also benefit greatly by using the <a target="_self" href="https://themainsmm.com/services"><strong style="font-weight: bold">best SMM panel for YouTube</strong></a>. Here are five tips for marketing your business on YouTube:</p>

<p><strong style="font-weight: bold">1. Create a channel and build an audience</strong></p>
<p>The first step to marketing your business on YouTube is creating a channel. A channel is like a home base where you can organize all of the videos, playlists, and other content that you create for your audience to find and watch.</p>

<p>You should also make sure that your channel is optimized for search so people can easily find it when they're looking for products like yours. This means creating good titles and descriptions for each video (more on this below), adding tags related to what you sell, and setting up an end card at the end of each video with links back to other relevant pages on your website or social media accounts (like Facebook) so viewers can learn more about how they might benefit from purchasing something similar in future--and sharing those videos across multiple platforms like Facebook groups related specifically toward topics relevant towards what type of product/service being offered by the business owner(s).</p>

<p><strong style="font-weight: bold">2. Optimize your video titles and tags</strong></p>
<p>The title and tags are the most important elements of your video. They're what viewers see first when searching for videos and can help you get noticed in search results.</p>
<p>The best titles are short, catchy, relevant, and include keywords that your audience is searching for. For example, if you were selling dog beds online, you might use "dog bed" or "dog beds" as part of your title: "Dog Beds That Are Comfortable For Dogs".</p>

<p>You also want to make sure your title isn't misleading--don't promise something in the title that isn't true. This will only hurt sales since customers won't trust anything else on your channel after seeing this type of false advertising (and they'll tell their friends).</p>

<p><strong style="font-weight: bold">3. Use annotations to drive viewers to other videos or your website</strong></p>
<p>Annotations are links that appear on the screen during a video. They can be clicked to take viewers to other videos or websites, and they're great for driving traffic to your website.</p>

<p>Annotations are text boxes that you add using the annotations editor in YouTube Studio (formerly Creator Studio). You can also add images with an annotation by uploading them directly into the editor, or by inserting one from Google Photos.</p>

<p><strong style="font-weight: bold">4. Live stream regularly</strong></p>
<p>Live streaming is a great way to connect with your audience and increase engagement. The live stream can be used as a preview for an upcoming video, or it can simply serve as an opportunity for you to interact directly with viewers.</p>

<p>Live streams are especially beneficial if you're trying to build up a following in a new niche or market. Your audience will appreciate that they get real-time updates from their favorite creators and get excited about what's coming next.</p>

<p>To ensure that your live streams go smoothly, make sure that both the quality of your internet connection and any equipment (like webcams) is adequate enough for streaming purposes before starting the broadcast. If possible, it's also advisable to have someone else present during these events so that they can help troubleshoot technical problems should they arise during recording time while also providing additional commentary if needed later down the line when editing the footage together into one cohesive video post after everything has been shot live online.</p>

<p><strong style="font-weight: bold">5. Integrate YouTube with other social media platforms</strong></p>
<ul>
  <li>Add a YouTube share button to your website and make it easy for viewers to share your videos on their social media accounts. You can use annotations in the video itself, or add them as annotations on individual screenshots of the video (which will show up when people click through).</li>
  <li>Use channel trailers: These are short videos that explain what you do and why people should watch your channel, as well as provide links back to other videos or websites. They're great for attracting new followers, who will then see more of what you have to offer if they keep coming back for more.</li>
  <li>Create playlists: Playlists allow viewers who love one type of content from a creator--like comedy sketches--to find similar content easily by grouping together related videos into one place on their homepage feed page.</li>
</ul>
